E2.evidence:
The defect is a time-of-check/time-of-use race between two methods of tf2::BufferCore inside one process: addTransformableRequest took transformable_requests_mutex_ only at the end, so a concurrent testTransformableRequests could run all callbacks before the new request was appended, and the request's callback was never called. It occurs with any correct caller; no value, frame, name or timing assumption between tf2_ros and tf2 is violated. Fix comment in the diff: "This is to
  // ensure that we don't have a TOCTTOU race between this method and
  // testTransformableRequests." and "We fix this by holding the mutex
  // across most of this method." -- https://github.com/ros2/geometry2/pull/449/files (tf2/src/buffer_core.cpp). The fix only moves the lock in tf2/src/buffer_core.cpp and drops a sleep in test_tf2/test/test_message_filter.cpp. Additional context (confidence in this eligibility decision: high): ROS 2 Foxy (ros:foxy docker). PR #449 (merged into the foxy branch as 2942156c3f1e61f33f7e60c6077b6572d168811a) is a backport of #307, which had fixed the same race on the rolling/galactic line (related issue #305). The issue was later closed as end-of-life by clalancette (https://github.com/ros2/geometry2/issues/377#issuecomment-2518753476), not by the PR. The flakiness is a test failure caused by an internal race in BufferCore; it is not an interface mismatch between tf2_ros and tf2.
